#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649977 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9705608-d638-5e5d-b560-a728314a7a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-104098" :question/problem "A line passes through the points  $P(-3,-1,4)$ and $Q(1,8,9).$ Find its vector equation in the form $r\\times v=c.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-3,-1,4)$ and $Q(1,8,9).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, starting with the equation of the line using the cross product, we manipulate the equation and then substitute in our values of $p$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v-p\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v & =p\\times v \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =-41i-31j+23k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
